          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caedō, caedere — \"to cut down, to kill; to beat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mīlitēs hostēs fugientēs caedēbant. The soldiers were cutting down the fleeing enemy."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caedō, caedere, cecīdī, caesum. Also to fell trees and to beat. Its noun is caedēs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "caedō is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to cut, to hew\" (EtymDB). English words in -cide, such as homicide, come from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
